{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgS7yqpagyab2cdjey4xh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed the generator. The watch was snapping each HR range to its own zones because of the name of one field. In each heart-rate target, `gen.py` was sending the lower bound as `value`. It now sends it as `start` (`{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}`). I haven't pushed anything or synced a watch, so whether the watch now shows the right ranges is still unconfirmed.\n\n**Why this was the cause:** Every planned range showed up as one of the watch's zones. For example, Float at 75–82% came out as 137–151, which is exactly the watch's Z3. Changing the watch's LTHR moved those bands, but the targets still landed on zone edges. The API had accepted the event as sent, so the problem was in how Intervals passes the target on to the watch. With `value`/`end`, the watch treats the target as a zone and maps it onto its own zones. With `start`/`end`, it shows the bpm range Intervals worked out from its own LTHR of 172.\n\nThe generator now writes `start` for all four HR steps and no `value` keys, and it still runs cleanly. I also added a short comment in `hr_target()` explaining why the field name matters.\n\n**To confirm:** regenerate the event, push it with `./push.sh`, read it back from the API, then sync the watch. The Float step should read 129–141 rather than 137–151. Because the watch's LTHR (168) differs from Intervals' (172), it'll be easy to tell which one the watch used.\n\n**What I used from the colleague's note** (details in `ANSWER.md`):\n- **Used:**\n  - The `start`-not-`value` finding. Your earlier failed attempts and LTHR numbers are the same as theirs.\n  - Their advice to keep percentages of LTHR rather than absolute bpm, which the generator already did.\n  - Their check of comparing the watch's numbers against Intervals' LTHR, not the watch's.\n- **Not used:** the part about adding